macOS: the user-mode callback return no longer writes LastErrorValue back a second time.

Patch 0011 writes PE's %gs:0x68 back into TEB->LastErrorValue at every entry into unix code, and it
did so in user_mode_callback_return too. That function is only ever reached through NtCallbackReturn,
a system call, so the syscall dispatcher's entry had already written PE's value to the TEB and put
libc's slot 13 back into %gs:0x68. The second write-back then copied libc's slot, a heap pointer,
into the TEB, and the next return to PE code handed that pointer to GetLastError().

Every window procedure and driver-load callback goes through that return, so the first display
call of a process poisoned LastError: lasterr3.exe on x64-crossover26.3-r12 and r13 (M4, macOS
27.0) reads GetLastError() = 60687104 after GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXSCREEN) and again after
CreateWindowExW, the same value both times, the thread's slot 13. r7, before 0011, reads 0. The
Rockstar Games Launcher installer checks GetLastError() right after it calculates the display DPI
and stops with "An error has occurred. Please make sure all instances of the Rockstar Games Launcher
have been closed" (its log: "Last error: 22725120 (0x15ac200): (unknown error)"), on both Steam's
first-run install of Red Dead Redemption 2 and Highball's own launcher recipe (highball-db#272,
2026-10-02). The QoS slot restore 0009 placed in the same function is idempotent and stays.

The host restore of slot 13 is dropped along with the write-back: %gs:0x68 already holds libc's
value there, for the same reason.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -1853,10 +1853,6 @@
                                                          NTSTATUS status, TEB *teb );
 __ASM_GLOBAL_FUNC( user_mode_callback_return,
 #ifdef __APPLE__
-                   "movl %gs:0x68,%r10d\n\t"       /* PE's LastErrorValue back into the TEB */
-                   "movl %r10d,0x68(%rcx)\n\t"
-                   "movq 0x348(%rcx),%r10\n\t"     /* host slot 13 back: unix code owns %gs:0x68 again */
-                   "movq %r10,%gs:0x68\n\t"
                    "movq 0x340(%rcx),%r10\n\t" /* amd64_thread_data()->host_qos: unix code owns %gs:0x20 again */
                    "movq %r10,%gs:0x20\n\t"
 #endif
